---
import { readFileSync, readdirSync } from 'node:fs';
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { Prose } from '../components/Prose';
import { SectionHeading } from '../components/SectionHeading';
import { Contribution } from '../components/Contribution';
import { ArrowLink } from '../components/Link';
import { hash } from '../../design/hash.mjs';

/**
 * Every number on this page is computed here, at build time, from the files it
 * describes. Nothing is typed in. If the system shrinks, the page says so.
 *
 * That is the point: the case study argues for a specification machines can
 * read, and a page about it that had to be hand-updated would be arguing
 * against itself.
 */
const tokens = JSON.parse(readFileSync('tokens/tokens.json', 'utf8'));
const banding = JSON.parse(readFileSync('design/banding-export.json', 'utf8'));

const leaves = (node: any): number =>
  Object.entries(node).reduce((n, [k, v]: [string, any]) => {
    if (k.startsWith('$')) return n;
    if (v && v.$value !== undefined) return n + 1;
    return v && typeof v === 'object' ? n + leaves(v) : n;
  }, 0);

const specDirs = ['components/specs', 'icons/specs', 'marks/specs'];
const specCount = specDirs.reduce(
  (n, d) => n + readdirSync(d).filter((f) => f.endsWith('.json')).length, 0);

const componentCount = readdirSync('src/components')
  .filter((f) => f.endsWith('.tsx') && !f.endsWith('.stories.tsx')).length;

const storyCount = readdirSync('src/components')
  .filter((f) => f.endsWith('.stories.tsx'))
  .reduce((n, f) => n + (readFileSync(`src/components/${f}`, 'utf8').match(/export const /g)?.length ?? 0), 0);

/** Recomputed here rather than quoted, using the same hash the verifiers use. */
const canonical = (v: any): string => {
  if (Array.isArray(v)) return `[${v.map(canonical).join(',')}]`;
  if (v && typeof v === 'object') {
    return `{${Object.keys(v).filter((k) => !k.startsWith('_')).sort()
      .map((k) => `${k}:${canonical(v[k])}`).join(',')}}`;
  }
  return JSON.stringify(v);
};

const facts = [
  { term: 'Design tokens', detail: `${leaves(tokens.primitive)} primitives and ${leaves(tokens.semantic.light)} semantic tokens per theme, exported from Figma and checksum verified against it.` },
  { term: 'Text styles', detail: `${leaves(tokens.typography)}, each emitted as CSS custom properties. Responsive type steps between two of them at a breakpoint rather than clamping, so every size on screen exists in the design file.` },
  { term: 'Components', detail: `${componentCount} in code, ${specCount} with a published contract. The ones with no Figma counterpart have their specs generated from their own TypeScript and stylesheets.` },
  { term: 'Stories', detail: `${storyCount}, every one rendered inside a band so the zero override property is checked the same way it is asserted in Figma. Each is snapshotted light and dark.` },
  { term: 'Layout rules', detail: `${banding.rules.length} band adjacency rules, read from the Figma page rather than transcribed. Checksum ${hash(canonical(banding))}.` }
];
---

<Page
  title="How this was built"
  standfirst="A design system where Figma, the code and the build agree, and where the build refuses to ship if they stop agreeing."
>
  <Prose lead>
    <p>
      The first case study on this site argues for a single written
      specification that designers, engineers and language models can all build
      from. That project ended before it reached production, so this site is the
      smallest complete version of the same idea: not a description of the
      approach, but the approach running, on something you can open.
    </p>
    <p>
      None of the numbers below were typed in. They are computed when this page
      is built, from the files they describe.
    </p>
  </Prose>

  <div class="hb-block">
    <Contribution items={facts} />
  </div>

  <div class="hb-block">
    <SectionHeading level={3}>What the build refuses to ship</SectionHeading>
    <Prose>
      <p>
        Four checks run on every push. Each exists because something got through
        without it.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Tokens.</strong> Every alias resolves, and the export
          checksum matches the one Figma reproduces from inside the file. A
          renamed variable fails the build instead of silently rendering nothing.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Components.</strong> Every value in every spec is a token
          reference. Zero literals, asserted rather than reviewed.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>CSS.</strong> Every custom property in the source resolves to
          a real token, and every font value binds to the type scale. A typo
          like <code>fg-secondry</code> is not a CSS error: it renders as an
          inherited default and looks deliberate, so nothing else would catch it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Bands.</strong> The built pages are linted against the layout
          rules, and those rules are read from Figma rather than copied. Adding
          a rule there fails the build here until someone implements it.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </Prose>
  </div>

  <div class="hb-block">
    <SectionHeading level={3}>What it does not do</SectionHeading>
    <Prose>
      <p>
        It does not check whether the design is any good. It checks that the
        design and the code are the same thing, which is a narrower claim and
        the only one worth automating. Taste is still done by looking.
      </p>
      <p>
        Three things live only in code, because Figma cannot express them:
        state, which is a CSS concern rather than a variant axis; press
        feedback; and required properties, where the type system enforces what a
        variant never could.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </div>

  <div class="hb-block">
    <ArrowLink href="/work/machine-readable-components">Read the case study this came from</ArrowLink>
  </div>
</Page>

<style is:global>
  .hb-block + .hb-block { margin-block-start: var(--primitive-layout-l64); }
</style>
